<!-- One generated student chickadee.

     Rendered from an AvatarPresentation passed as a SUB-CONTEXT
     (Core/AvatarMarkup.swift). The sub-context form makes the passed object
     this partial's root, so its fields are addressed flat here - qualifying
     them with the caller's field name yields empty strings, silently, and a
     bird with no colours and no wing still renders and still returns 200.
     which carries palette TOKEN NAMES rather than colours and the wing symbol
     rather than any geometry. The five use elements below are the whole bird,
     back to front; their symbols live in the avatar sprite partial, which the
     page must also include - once per page, not once per bird.

     The style attribute is a list of custom-property assignments and nothing
     else, which is what keeps it inside the inline-style allowlist. Note the
     cheek token appears twice on purpose: the marks on a patterned wing take
     the cheek colour so a pattern reads against any cap family.

     A page shows a bird beside a handle or a name most of the time, in which
     case it is decorative and the text beside it carries the identity. Where
     the bird stands alone it must announce whose it is, which is what the
     labelled branch is for. The caller decides, because only the caller can
     see what is next to it. -->
#if(isLabelled):
<svg class="#(sizeClass)" viewBox="0 0 64 64" role="img" aria-label="#(label)"
     style="--av-cap: var(#(capToken)); --av-wing: var(#(wingToken)); --av-beak: var(#(beakToken)); --av-cheek: var(#(cheekToken)); --av-wing-mark: var(#(cheekToken)); --av-flank: var(#(flankToken)); --av-backdrop: var(#(backdropToken))">
#else:
<svg class="#(sizeClass)" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"
     style="--av-cap: var(#(capToken)); --av-wing: var(#(wingToken)); --av-beak: var(#(beakToken)); --av-cheek: var(#(cheekToken)); --av-wing-mark: var(#(cheekToken)); --av-flank: var(#(flankToken)); --av-backdrop: var(#(backdropToken))">
#endif
    <use href="#av-backdrop"/>
    <use href="#av-plumage"/>
    <use href="#(wingSymbolRef)"/>
    <use href="#av-beak"/>
    <use href="#av-eyes"/>
</svg>
